2. Outcome 1 — Chat Experience and Evaluation Framework 

Deliver a first, demoable prototype version of the chat application for internal stakeholders and designated reviewers. 

Define the initial feature set that is expected to be working by the end of the code-a-thon. 

Create a first version of an evaluation framework for content validation, response quality, and experiment measurement. 

Produce a one-page summary of the evaluation approach so we can clearly state what we measured and how the prototype performed against those measures. 

Define the initial experiments either as pre-work or during Day 1. The goal is to start learning while we are building—not wait until the prototype is complete. 

Use this outcome to establish the repeatable patterns for experimentation that can be carried into later iterations. 

3. Outcome 4 — Pre-Surgery Care Companion 

Entry point: The prototype experience begins when a surgery is scheduled. 

The companion supports the period from surgery scheduling through the surgery itself. 

It should answer questions and provide education that help the patient prepare for the upcoming surgery. 

General appointment preparation that occurs before a surgery is scheduled is out of scope for this outcome. 

The scheduled surgery acts as the trigger that starts the “preparing for surgery” experience and defines the patient’s position in the journey. 

The code-a-thon target is a functional prototype for a specific surgery use case—not a complete solution for every perioperative scenario. 

4. Outcome 5 — Post-Surgery / “Going Home” Companion 

Entry point: The prototype experience begins once the surgery has been completed. 

Support the patient’s common questions and information needs after surgery, including the transition home and early recovery period. 

For this first version, assume a single completed surgery and no additional surgery currently on file, even though real patients may have multiple procedures. 

Use the draft post-surgery interface as the starting point for the functional prototype. 

Meds-to-Beds integration is explicitly out of scope for this code-a-thon and should be considered in a subsequent iteration. 

5. Additional Foundational enablers Code-a-thon Outcomes — Data and Experiment Interfaces 

Voice of Customer interface 

Create an initial interface that makes Voice of Customer data accessible for exploration and analysis. 

Complete a first-pass analysis to identify themes, recurring needs, friction points, and signals that could influence Outcomes 1, 4, and 5. 

Where feasible, test how Voice of Customer data can be linked to ROOT or the broader data foundation; full production integration can continue after the code-a-thon. 

Call-transcript data interface 

Create an initial interface for available call-transcript data so teams can explore the questions, concerns, and support needs expressed through phone interactions. 

Use prepared or available transcript data for an initial analysis and compare the themes with what we see in inbox messages. 

The code-a-thon outcome is the interface and initial learning loop; full call-center ingestion, history, and production integration can mature in later iterations. 

Experiment portfolio and results interface 

Create a single interface for the experiments we have run or defined to date and the data/results associated with them. 

Capture the hypothesis, experiment design, measures, status, results, conclusions, and supporting data or links where available. 

Use this as the beginning of a durable experiment record so teams can understand what has been tested, what was learned, and what should be tested next. 

6. Scope Boundaries — What This Is and Is Not 

This is a deliberately narrow first prototype  version designed to prove/ experiment the experience, data patterns, and technical approach. 

The objective is a functional prototype that can be evaluated and iterated—not the product. 

The initial production prototype use is not patient-facing. It is intended for controlled evaluation by physicians, clinical teams, and care teams using live data to get feedback.  

Using live data allows reviewers to compare the experience and evaluate real-world complexity that is difficult to reproduce with synthetic data alone. 

A first interface for call-transcript data is in scope. Full call-center history ingestion and production-grade integration are not required for this code-a-thon and can continue as the data pipeline matures. 

Complex multi-surgery journeys are not addressed in the first version of Outcome 5. 

7. Data We Will Use 

Primary source: Inbox messages 

Use patient inbox messages and the responses provided by physicians, nurses, clinics, or other care-team members as the primary source for question-and-answer patterns. 

Focus on messages associated with preparation for surgery and the period after surgery. 

Use these messages to identify recurring questions, gaps in education, and the content the companion should be able to provide. 

Journey/state source: ROOT 

Use ROOT as the source for understanding where a patient is in the journey and what events or states indicate that position. 

Use the data to define the practical boundaries for “pre-surgery” and “post-surgery” cohorts and to determine when the experience should activate. 

Day 1 should include enough analysis of this data to understand what is available, what can be reliably inferred, and what questions require additional work. 

Voice of Customer data 

Expose available Voice of Customer data through an initial interface and perform a first-pass analysis of themes and signals relevant to the experience. 

Connecting this data into ROOT or a broader production data foundation remains a stretch goal; the interface and initial analysis are code-a-thon outcomes. 

Call-transcript data 

Expose available or prepared call-transcript data through an initial interface and use it to identify patient questions, concerns, and recurring support needs. 

Compare transcript-derived themes with inbox-derived themes to understand where the channels reinforce each other and where they reveal different needs. 

Treat full call-center data integration and production ingestion as follow-on work if the source pipeline is not yet complete. 

Experiment data and results 

Bring together experiments and experiment data/results to date into a common interface that can be reviewed during and after the code-a-thon. 

Make the experiment record easy to understand: hypothesis, setup, measures, status, results, conclusions, and supporting evidence where available. 

8. Discovery, Insight, and Experiment Activities 

Important framing: For this code-a-thon, “discovery agent,” “insight agent,” and “experiment agent” are best treated as work patterns or activities that support the product outcomes—not necessarily as three separate products that must be completed. 

Activity 

What we will do 

How it supports the outcomes 

Discovery 

Explore ROOT and related data; define the problem statement, journey stages, and entry/exit boundaries. 

Clarifies who belongs in the pre-surgery and post-surgery cohorts and when each experience should activate. 

Insights 

Analyze inbox content to identify recurring questions, themes, content gaps, and opportunities. 

Shapes the questions, answers, education, and interaction design for Outcomes 4 and 5. 

Experiments 

Define hypotheses, evaluation criteria, and measures; run targeted experiments against the chat/prototype. 

Creates the learning loop for Outcome 1 and provides evidence for what should be improved next. 

9. Production Evaluation Approach 

Plan to take the Outcome 4 and Outcome 5 prototypes into a production environment so they can be evaluated against live patient data. 

Limit access to designated physicians, clinical teams, and care teams for evaluation and validation. 

Do not expose the experience directly to patients during this phase. 

Use the production evaluation to understand whether the responses, journey logic, and data interpretation make sense in real clinical workflows and when compared with Epic. 

Capture findings and use them to define the next product iteration and additional integrations. 

10. Day-by-Day Working Plan 

Day 1 — Tuesday: Define, Discover, and Align 

Introduction to evaluations — a practical primer on what evaluations are, why they matter for these experiences, how to define useful measures, and how to apply them during the code-a-thon. 

Discovery and Insights — a primer on the existing data landscape, with a guided look at Inbox data and ROOT data, what each source tells us, and how to use them to frame the experience and identify opportunities. 

