12. Key Messages to Reinforce in the Room 

We are building a real first prototype version, not a slideware concept—but it is intentionally scoped to a specific use case. 

The key journey boundary is simple: Outcome 4 starts when surgery is scheduled; Outcome 5 starts when surgery is completed. 

Inbox data tells us what patients are asking; ROOT helps us understand where the patient is in the journey. 

Discovery, insights, and experiments are the learning mechanisms that support the products we are building. 

The Voice of Customer, call-transcript, and experiment interfaces are enabling assets: they make the evidence and learning behind the experiences visible, reusable, and easier to act on. 

The first production evaluation is clinician/care-team facing, using live data; it is not a patient-facing launch. 

Success at the end of the code-a-thon is both a working prototype and a clearer, evidence-based path for the next iteration. 

Suggested close: “At the end of the code-a-thon, we want working first prototype versions of the experiences, a clear way to evaluate them, and practical interfaces into Voice of Customer, call-transcript, and experiment evidence—so we leave with both functioning prototypes and a stronger, evidence-based foundation for what we should build and test next.” 

 
